---
import ComparePage from '../../components/ComparePage.astro';
---

<ComparePage
  competitor="Docker Compose"
  title="BridgePort vs Docker Compose"
  description="Docker Compose is perfect for one host. BridgePort adds the fleet layer: central deploys, encrypted secrets, health checks, monitoring, and rollback across all your servers."
  framing="Fleet control plane vs single-host tooling"
  intro={[
    'This one is less a rivalry than a stack: Docker Compose is a great way to define what runs on a host, and BridgePort keeps using it — services can carry Compose templates that BridgePort renders and ships to each server. The comparison that matters is operating one host by hand versus operating a fleet through a control plane.',
    'Compose alone scales beautifully until the second server. Then deploys become SSH sessions, secrets become scattered .env files, and "is everything healthy?" becomes a terminal habit. BridgePort centralizes exactly those parts and leaves Docker underneath untouched.',
  ]}
  rows={[
    {
      label: 'Scope',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Every server in every environment, from one place' },
      other: { mark: 'partial', text: 'One host at a time, driven from that host (or docker contexts by hand)' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Compose files',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Supported — services carry Compose templates, rendered and synced per server' },
      other: { mark: 'yes', text: 'The native format' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Deploys',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Central, repeatable, audited; multi-service plans with ordering and auto-rollback' },
      other: { mark: 'partial', text: 'docker compose up per host — ordering and rollback are on you' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Secrets',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'AES-256-GCM encrypted at rest, referenced from config, usage-tracked' },
      other: { mark: 'no', text: 'Plaintext .env files on each host' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Config management',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Templated config files with reusable fragments, synced to servers' },
      other: { mark: 'no', text: 'Copy files around yourself' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Monitoring & health',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Metrics, container/URL/TCP/TLS health checks, notifications' },
      other: { mark: 'partial', text: 'healthcheck: exists per container; no metrics, alerting, or history' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Web UI & visibility',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Dashboard, topology diagram, logs, audit trail' },
      other: { mark: 'no', text: 'CLI only' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Team access',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'Users, roles, service accounts, scoped API tokens, audit log' },
      other: { mark: 'no', text: 'Everyone with SSH is root-equivalent on that host' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Automation',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'REST API, CLI, Go SDK, Terraform provider, CI/CD webhooks' },
      other: { mark: 'partial', text: 'Scriptable, but you build the automation yourself' },
    },
    {
      label: 'Footprint',
      bp: { mark: 'yes', text: 'One extra container total (not per host)' },
      other: { mark: 'yes', text: 'None beyond Docker itself' },
    },
  ]}
  chooseBp={[
    'You run services on two or more servers (or will soon).',
    'More than one person deploys, and you want an audit trail instead of shared SSH keys.',
    'Secrets currently live in .env files and that quietly bothers you.',
    'You want health checks, metrics, and alerts without assembling a monitoring stack.',
  ]}
  chooseOther={[
    'Everything you run fits on one server and you are the only operator.',
    'It is local development or a homelab where a UI and audit trail add nothing.',
    'You want absolutely minimal moving parts and SSH-and-edit is a feature, not a bug.',
  ]}
  reviewed="July 2026"
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Does BridgePort replace Docker Compose?',
      a: 'No — it builds on it. Services in BridgePort can carry a Docker Compose template; BridgePort renders it per deployment, ships it to each server, and runs docker compose up for you, adding ordering, health gates, and rollback on top.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can BridgePort import what my compose files already run?',
      a: 'Yes. Container discovery imports the containers already running on a server as services, so you can adopt a compose-managed host without stopping anything.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What does BridgePort add on a single server?',
      a: 'Encrypted secrets, health checks with alerting, metrics history, config templating, an audit log, and a UI. On exactly one host with one operator that may be more than you need — Compose alone is a fine answer there.',
    },
  ]}
/>
